- Acceptance: `document.documentElement.scrollWidth <= innerWidth` on every admin screen at 320 to 768, at 200% zoom and in landscape.

## 2.2 No mobile navbar showing
Likely causes to check, in this order:
1. **Breakpoint mismatch.** `BottomTabBar` and the mobile header controls use `md:hidden` while the desktop controls (Scan, account menu, New Meeting) are `hidden lg:*`, and `main` only reserves bottom padding below `sm` (`pb-24 sm:pb-8`). Between 640 and 1023 px content can sit under the bar, or controls vanish entirely (no Scan button, no account/sign-out, no More sheet). Make the two sets of breakpoints exactly complementary (choose one switch point, e.g. `lg`, and use it everywhere: navbar, bottom bar, More sheet, padding, comments).
2. **Render conditions.** Check in `App.tsx` that `BottomTabBar` (rendered near the end of the shell) is rendered for every signed-in state that should have it, including admins without a linked roster record and members in every membership phase that shows the shell. Also check the guest Public Hub: does it have a mobile navigation at all? If not, give it the same bottom bar pattern (guest tabs only).
3. **Fixed-position ancestors.** A `position: fixed` bar disappears or is mispositioned if any ancestor has `transform`, `filter`, `backdrop-filter`, `contain` or `will-change: transform` (this includes elements wrapped by animation libraries). Keep `BottomTabBar` and `MobileMoreSheet` outside any such wrapper (render them as direct children of the root app container). Also check `overflow` and `z-index` stacking against modals and the skip link.
4. **Keyboard handling.** The keyboard-aware hide logic (`visualViewport`) was removed in the latest version; with a fixed bar, the on-screen keyboard can cover or push the bar over inputs. Restore: hide the bar while any text input is focused or the visual viewport shrinks, and show it again on blur.
5. **Safe areas.** Use `padding-bottom: env(safe-area-inset-bottom)` and `viewport-fit=cover`; confirm on iOS Safari and Android Chrome.
- Acceptance: at 320, 390, 768 and 1023 px a signed-in user always has primary navigation (bottom bar or navbar), a Scan entry, account/sign-out, notifications and the More sheet; at 1024 px and above the desktop navbar has all of those too. Test as member, as admin, as admin with no linked roster record, and as guest.

## 2.3 "Sign in to check in" button on the public leaderboard does not work in Chrome
Findings already visible in the code (verify them):
- `PublicLeaderboardView` renders the button (`aria-label="Sign in to check in"`) with `onClick={onOpenLogin}`.
- In `App.tsx` the guest hub passes `onOpenLogin={() => setShowGuestLeaderboard(false)}`, which only switches back to the login screen (it does not start sign-in and may leave `guestTab`/hash state behind), and the signed-in leaderboard tab passes **`onOpenLogin={() => {}}`, a no-op**.
- `signInWithGoogle` uses `signInWithPopup`.
Fix and investigate:
1. Make the button reliably start sign-in: from the guest hub it should **directly call `signInWithGoogle()` inside the click handler** (no `await` or state change before the call, otherwise Chrome's popup blocker blocks it), then land the user on the check-in/home flow; if the user already has the guest hub open, clear guest mode and hash state cleanly. Replace the no-op with the same behavior or hide the button when a user exists.
2. Add the fallback: on `auth/popup-blocked`, `auth/operation-not-supported-in-this-environment` or a popup that closes instantly, fall back to `signInWithRedirect` (and handle `getRedirectResult` on load), and show a clear message otherwise. Do not swallow errors; map Firebase auth error codes to short user text.
3. Check the likely Chrome-specific causes and report which one applies: popup blocked; `auth/unauthorized-domain` because `dob-hub.ai.studio` (and the Cloud Run URL) is not in Firebase Authentication → Settings → Authorized domains; third-party storage partitioning when the app runs inside an iframe; `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy` header interfering with the popup (the server sets none today; do not add COOP without testing the popup); the button covered by another element (`z-index`, fixed header, `pointer-events`) or a `disabled` state; the click handler attached to the wrong element.
4. After sign-in the pending deep-link check-in (`?s=...&t=...`) must resume (see Phase 4, D3).
- Acceptance: tested in Chrome desktop, Chrome Android, Safari iOS and Firefox, both on `dob-hub.ai.studio` and in the AI Studio preview; the button either signs the user in or shows a specific, actionable message; no silent failures.

